WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.370
- [Narrator] This penny
isn't worth 1 cent.

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:06.690
It's worth 10 billion at
least for this video, okay?

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.250
And it's going into the Treasury

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.200
to be spent on an entire year

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.623
of the Environmental Protection Agency.

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:19.350
Here's how much went to
unemployment and NASA.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:24.150
Last year, more than six
trillion, or 627 pennies,

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.190
were spent on different programs,

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:30.183
but that required taking on
more than $1 trillion in debt.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.540
Let's go to the board to
look at federal spending,

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:34.830
including that debt,

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.503
and the ways the U.S.
could borrow fewer pennies.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.890
But first, where did all the pennies spent

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.290
in 2022 come from?

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.540
Here's how many came from revenue.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:47.700
Things like individual income taxes

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.120
and payroll taxes and corporate taxes.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.120
Congress is responsible
for both these totals.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.870
They set the tax rate and
they authorize the spending,

00:00:57.870 --> 00:00:59.820
but since these don't match up,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.940
the Treasury takes on debt
to cover this difference.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:07.080
They do that by selling securities
like say, a 20-year bond.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.910
An investor gives the Treasury money

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:10.860
and the Treasury would
pay interest on that

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.750
every six months.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.270
They'll do that until the 20 years is over

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.940
and then the investor
will get their money back.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.430
But those interest payments add up.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.050
- That is one of the expenses

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.970
that people who worry about
the debt and the deficit

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.110
like to focus on the most,

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.790
or they see as one of
the biggest problems,

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:30.660
is how much does the US
government pay on an annual basis

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.030
just on paying its previous debts?

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.910
- [Narrator] In 2022, it was a lot.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:40.650
8.5% of all spending went to
paying interest on that debt.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.870
- The concern is that
so much money over time

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:44.310
will start going just towards the debt

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:45.360
that you may not be able to spend

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.100
what you wanna spend on other issues.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:48.540
- [Narrator] Here are the other places

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.880
the U.S. spent money in 2022.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.810
This biggest section here
is all Social Security.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:58.170
These sections, Medicare and Medicaid.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:01.740
This third biggest
section is the military.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.930
Then there's the Department of Education.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.940
Most of this is student loans,

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.600
the cost of Biden's potential
student loan forgiveness plan.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.240
There's tax credits like child credits,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.230
Veterans Affairs, and
nutritional services, like SNAP.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.440
The rest of this other
category went to smaller places

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.220
like Coronavirus relief, the
Department of Transportation,

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.350
or Homeland Security

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.900
- Beyond Medicare, Social Security,

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:31.050
interest payments on the
debt, and the Pentagon budget,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.580
everything you think of in terms of

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.100
what the federal government
does on an annual basis

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.350
is actually a a small minority

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:40.140
of actual annual government spending.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.350
- [Narrator] In 2022, more
than 20% of that spending

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.363
was funded by taking on debt.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.710
This is the total amount of
debt built up every year.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.040
It's currently a little over $30 trillion,

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.680
and there's a cap to how much this can be.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.360
The infamous-
- Debt ceiling.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.740
- Debt ceiling.
- Debt ceiling.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:01.800
- [Narrator] That limit
is currently 31 trillion.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.770
An amount that the Treasury
is set to hit in June.

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:06.480
When that happens, they'll be unable

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.270
to take on extra debt
through those securities,

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.920
which means there won't be enough cash

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.900
to pay Social Security benefits.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.940
Seniors won't get their checks,

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.520
neither would members of the military,

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.850
and these debt holders wouldn't
get their interest payments.

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.500
- That would create all sorts

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.390
of financial and economic problems.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.970
Because U.S. treasuries are viewed

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:28.530
as kind of this safe haven asset

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.600
for the global financial system,

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.857
there's this idea that if
suddenly investors thought,

00:03:32.857 --> 00:03:35.250
"Well, if I buy U.S. government debt,

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:36.930
I might not get my money back,"

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.270
that there could be a sell
off in U.S. government bonds

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:42.270
and that would raise interest rates

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:44.400
across the world economy.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.200
- [Narrator] It's up to
Congress to pass a bill,

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.700
that the president then signs,

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.250
to raise this limit so
that doesn't happen.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:53.040
Both parties agree this
chart should go down,

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.470
but they disagree on how to do that.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.690
- If you're trying to reduce the deficit

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.820
or eventually even reduce the debt,

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.360
it is to either cut
spending or to raise taxes.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.490
- [Narrator] Many Democrats
want to raise taxes

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:07.770
on the rich and corporations.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:11.013
- I wanna make it clear,
I'm gonna raise some taxes.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:13.770
Many of you who're billionaires out there,

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:15.593
you're gonna stop paying at 3%.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:17.760
- [Narrator] While Republicans

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.920
largely want to see spending cuts,

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.060
and they want those cuts

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.850
as a condition for
raising the debt ceiling.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.770
Kevin McCarthy even promised to do it

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:27.930
as part of his deal to become Speaker.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.900
- The responsible debt limit increase

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:34.260
that puts us on a path
towards a healthier economy.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.090
- [Narrator] But Democrats
want just a clean raise

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:38.820
to the debt limit to be
able to pay for the spending

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.650
Congress already approved.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:45.120
- It is something that should
be done without conditions.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.760
We should not be negotiating around it.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.320
- [Narrator] So if there were cuts,

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:50.820
where might they come from?

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.800
Cuts to Social Security and Medicare

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.540
seem to not be an option.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.547
- Social Security, Medicare
is off the books now, right?

00:04:57.547 --> 00:05:02.547
They're not be start. We got unanimity.

00:05:02.603 --> 00:05:04.110
- [Narrator] And not all Republicans

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:05.940
support cuts to the military.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.430
- I think there's room for
savings in the military too.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:10.290
- We've gotta get spending under control

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:13.410
but we are not gonna do it
on the backs of our troops.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.390
- [Narrator] Which only
leaves these departments

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:18.540
and discretionary programs
as places for cuts.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.580
- It's safe to say that marginal cuts,

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:21.810
while you know something

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:23.940
that may be worth
considering in various ways,

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.670
and whether it's a wise use
of resources to begin with,

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:27.960
are not necessarily something

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.280
that's gonna make a major difference

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.020
towards reducing a deficit

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:33.780
or, you know, balancing
the budget, for example.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:35.310
- [Narrator] While
Democrats and Republicans

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.380
may disagree how to do that,

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:40.654
they both agree that the U.S.
can't let the pennies stop.

00:05:40.654 --> 00:05:43.321
(upbeat music)

